---
title: "Agent profile"
description: "Configure the name and avatar your project presents to the people it messages."
---

```text
photon project agent-profile
├── view
├── edit [--first-name] [--last-name | --clear-last-name] [--idempotency-key <key>]
└── avatar
    ├── upload <file> [--idempotency-key <key>]
    └── reset [--yes] [--idempotency-key <key>]
```

<Note>
  Members and invitations belong to the organization. See
  [Organizations](/cli/organizations#members).
</Note>

The agent profile is the name and avatar your project presents to the people it
messages.

```sh
photon project agent-profile view
photon project agent-profile edit --first-name Ada --last-name Lovelace
photon project agent-profile edit --clear-last-name
```

With `--json`, `view`, `edit`, `avatar upload` and `avatar reset` print the
agent profile as Photon returns it, with every field in it, including a field
the API adds after this CLI release:

```json
{"avatarUrl":"https://images.example.com/agent.png","firstName":"Ada","lastName":"Lovelace"}
```

## Avatar

```sh
photon project agent-profile avatar upload ./agent.webp
photon project agent-profile avatar reset --yes
```

Upload accepts a regular JPEG, PNG, or WebP file. The CLI detects the media
type from the file's signature rather than its extension, requests a presigned
upload form, uploads the file without a Photon authorization header, and
commits only once storage accepts it. It prints the profile Photon returns
when it commits the upload. Presigned URLs and form values are never printed.

An unresolved pre-commit upload keeps a private copy of the validated image so
a replay can finish without you supplying the file again.
